{:edb/db-before-t 138 :edb/db-after-t 139 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533451 50 #inst "2026-10-10T22:37:21.865Z" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064026 1071 "A cube is shrinking uniformly at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm}^3/\\text{s}$. How fast is each side of the cube shrinking when the volume of the cube is $27\\,\\text{mm}^3$?\n\n![](images/0c/0ca7021995b1a501f6cb862b33649bdb1927f7ce4a8630a9e261382a193f40a4.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186064026 1071 "A cube is shrinking uniformly at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm}^3/\\text{s}.$ How fast is each side of the cube shrinking when the volume of the cube is $27\\,\\text{mm}^3?$\n\n![](images/0c/0ca7021995b1a501f6cb862b33649bdb1927f7ce4a8630a9e261382a193f40a4.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064026 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and $V$ be its volume. We are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{dV}{dt} =-3mm^{3}/s\n$$\n\nWe would like to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nV=27\\,\\text{mm}^3\n$$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n$V=x^3$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}V &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 3x^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when\n\n$$\nV = 27\\,\\text{mm}^3\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= x^{3} \\\\\n27 &= x^{3} \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=3$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm dt}=-3\n$$\n\ninto the expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 3x^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n- 3 &= 3(3)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -\\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are shrinking at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nat the moment when\n\n$$\nx=3\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186064026 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V=27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV=x^3\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V = 27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 27 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm dt}=-3$ into the expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =3(3)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are shrinking at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{9}\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=3\\,\\textrm{mm}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064027 1071 "All of the edges of a cube are increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$. How fast is the surface area of the cube changing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,\\text{cm}^3$?\n\n![](images/a6/a6ef2af1ed8e44f82469625e232ed67c0b809a3c8199a6ce52fd2bf2e3fcffa7.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186064027 1071 "All of the edges of a cube are increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the surface area of the cube changing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,\\text{cm}^3?$\n\n![](images/a6/a6ef2af1ed8e44f82469625e232ed67c0b809a3c8199a6ce52fd2bf2e3fcffa7.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064027 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2mm/s\n$$\n\nWe would like to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d S}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nV= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$. So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS= 6x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}S &= \\frac{d}{dt}(6x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dS}{dt} &= 12x\\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when\n\n$$\nV= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= x^{3} \\\\\n8 &= x^{3} \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nx=2\\,\\text{cm}=20\\,\\text{mm}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dS}{dt} &= 12x\\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dS}{dt} &= 12(20)(2) \\\\\n\\frac{dS}{dt} &= 480\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of $480 \\,\\text{mm}^2/\\text{s}$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186064027 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d S}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS= 6x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}S & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2\\,\\text{cm}=20\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe can now plug the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(20)(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =480.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of  $480 \\,\\text{mm}^2/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064028 1071 "Water is being poured into a rectangular prism-shaped tank. The length and width of this tank are $20 \\textrm{ ft}$ and $15 \\textrm{ ft},$ respectively. If the height of the water increases at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{ft/min}$, determine the rate at which the volume of stored water increases.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7c6749ec0585ab714640fa9155eba212e2a9c628b50cc3c3de07492b8b3aec.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064028 1071 "Water is being poured into a rectangular prism-shaped tank. The length and width of this tank are $20 \\textrm{ft}$ and $15 \\textrm{ft}$, respectively. If the height of the water increases at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{ft/min}$, determine the rate at which the volume of stored water increases.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7c6749ec0585ab714640fa9155eba212e2a9c628b50cc3c3de07492b8b3aec.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186064028 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=20\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  W=15\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of the water in the tank:\n\n$$\nV = LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(20)(15)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =300\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is rising at a rate of $50\\,\\text{ft}^3\\textrm{/min}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064028 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=20\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad W=15\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h$, is given by the formula for the volume of the water in the tank:\n$V = LWh$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$, treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(V) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(LWh) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= LW\\frac{dh}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= (20)(15)\\frac{dh}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 300 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is rising at a rate of $50\\,\\text{ft}^3\\textrm{/min}$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373497 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375064 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375079 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375082 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375166 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373518 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373525 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375067 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375085 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375536 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375022 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375025 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375543 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374572 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374600 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374620 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375550 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373686 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375028 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375031 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375034 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062419 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186064732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374607 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064732 1071 "![](images/d2/d2be88ec9cb7559ca8d80d528e143d4f2dd72109c9dc576b353dd352855c53ff.png)\n\nA cube is expanding uniformly at a rate of $57cm^{3}/s$. How fast is each edge of the cube increasing when the volume of the cube is $27cm^{3}$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186064732 1071 "![](images/d2/d2be88ec9cb7559ca8d80d528e143d4f2dd72109c9dc576b353dd352855c53ff.png)\n\nA cube is expanding uniformly at a rate of $57{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is each edge of the cube increasing when the volume of the cube is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{3}?$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186064732 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=57\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=27\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=27\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 27 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=3$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=57$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 57 & =3(3)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{19}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are increasing at a rate of $\\frac{19}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=3\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186083000 1071 "Evaluate the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{8} 3 (2)^{n-1}$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186083000 1071 "Evaluate the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{8} 3 (2)^{n-1}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186083000 1074 "Since the given series is in the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N a_1 r^{n-1}}\n$$\n\nit is a geometric series.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1 = 3$, $r = 2$, and $N = 8$. If we substitute these values into the formula for $S_N$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(8)3(2)^{n - 1} &= 3 \\cdot (\\frac{1 - 2^{8}}{1 - 2}) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (\\frac{-255}{-1}) \\\\\n&= 765\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186083000 1074 "Since the given series is in the form $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N a_1 r^{n-1}},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1 = 3,$ $r = 2,$ and $N = 8.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for $S_N,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}3(2)^{n-1} & =3\\cdot (\\frac{1-{2}^{8}}{1-2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\frac{-255}{-1}) \\\\  & =765\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186083001 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{5} 3 (4)^{n}$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186083001 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{5} 3 (4)^{n}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186083001 1074 "Since the given series is in the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N a r^{n}}\n$$\n\nit is a geometric series.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r = 4$ and $N = 5$. We need to figure out the value of $a_1$.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\na_n = 3(4)^n\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term $a_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 3(4)^{1} \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $a_1 = 12$, $r = 4$, and $N = 5$ into the formula for $S_N$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(5)3(4)^{n} &= 12 \\cdot ((1 - 4^{5}))/(1 - 4) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{12}{3}(-1023) \\\\\n&= 4092\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186083001 1074 "Since the given series is in the form $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N a r^{n}},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r = 4$ and $N = 5.$ We need to figure out the value of $a_1.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\na_n = 3(4)^n,\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term $a_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =3(4)^{1} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $a_1 = 12,$ $r = 4,$ and $N = 5$ into the formula for $S_N,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}3(4)^{n} & =12\\cdot \\frac{(1-{4}^{5})}{1-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{3}(-1\\,023) \\\\  & =4\\,092\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186083002 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=0}^{4} 3\\left(2\\right)^{n-1} .$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186083002 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=0}^{4} 3\\left(2\\right)^{n-1}$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186083002 1074 "Since the given series is in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum{a_1 r^{n-1}}$, it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1$.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_0\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nNote that we use $a_0$ in the sum formula because $a_0$ is the first term of the series.\n\nIn this case, $r =2$. Also, note that $N=5$, since there are $5$ terms in total: $a_0$, $a_1$, $a_2$, $a_3$, $a_4$.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\na_n = 3\\left(2\\right)^{n-1}\n$$\n\nso the first term $a_0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{0} &= 3(2)^{0 - 1} \\\\\n&= 3(2)^{-1} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute\n\n$$\na_0 =\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\n$r=2$, and $N=5$ into the formula for $S_N$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{5} &= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot ((1 - 2^{5}))/((1 - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot ((-31))/((-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{93}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186083002 1074 "Since the given series is in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum{a_1 r^{n-1}},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_0\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nNote that we use $a_0$ in the sum formula because $a_0$ is the first term of the series.\n\nIn this case, $r =2.$ Also, note that $N=5,$ since there are $5$ terms in total: $a_0,$ $a_1,$ $a_2,$ $a_3,$ $a_4.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\na_n = 3\\left( 2\\right)^{n-1},\n$$\n\nso the first term $a_0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =3{(2)}^{0-1} \\\\  & =3{(2)}^{-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $a_0 =\\dfrac{3}{2},$ $r=2,$ and $N=5$ into the formula