/ref 17592186373679 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374537 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374613 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374623 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374630 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375070 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375073 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375076 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375348 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375173 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373532 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373539 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373791 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186105708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374565 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186105708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374637 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186105709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374544 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186114967 1071 "A vat initially holds $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of a sugar solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar.\nA sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar per liter is poured into the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, while the completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. What differential equation describes the amount of sugar $Q$, in kilograms, in the vat $t$ minutes after the inflow begins?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186114967 1071 "A vat initially holds $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of a sugar solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar.\nA sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar per liter is poured into the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ What differential equation describes the amount of sugar $Q,$ in kilograms, in the vat $t$ minutes after the inflow begins?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186114967 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t$, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t$, in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that a sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (0.30\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (18\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= (0.30\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (18\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= 5.4\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t$.\n\n- The vat initially contains $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n- Sugar solution enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the vat.\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, after $t$ minutes, $30t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the vat.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\n360 + 18t - 30t = (360-12t)\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (30\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= (30\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{30Q}{360 - 12t}\\frac{kg}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5Q}{60 - 2t}\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 5.4 - \\frac{5Q}{60 - 2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{5}{60 - 2t}Q = 5.4\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186114967 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that a sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =5.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t.$\n\n- The vat initially contains $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Sugar solution enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the vat.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $30t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the vat.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n360 + 18t - 30t = (360-12t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =5.4-\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{60-2t}Q=5.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186114968 1071 "A reactor initially holds $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of a chemical solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved reactant.\nPure solvent is pumped into the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$, while the completely mixed chemical solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent pumping begins." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186114968 1071 "A reactor initially holds $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of a chemical solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved reactant.\nPure solvent is pumped into the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed chemical solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent pumping begins." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186114968 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t$. Then, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t$, in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nNote that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$. So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (0\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\cdot (14\\frac{gal}{min}) \\\\\n&= (0\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\cdot (14\\frac{gal}{min}) \\\\\n&= 0\\frac{lb}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t$.\n\nThe reactor initially contains $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of chemical solution; solvent enters at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and drains out at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$.\nAs a result, the amount of chemical solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\n300 + 14t - 14t = 300\\,\\textrm{gal}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{300}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{lb}}{\\textrm{gal}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed chemical solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$, *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (14\\frac{gal}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\\\\n&= (14\\frac{gal}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{14Q}{300}\\frac{lb}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7Q}{150}\\frac{lb}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 - \\frac{7Q}{150}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{7}{150}\\,Q\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} &= -\\frac{7}{150} \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt &= \\int(-\\frac{7}{150})dt \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot dQ &= -\\frac{7}{150}\\int1dt \\\\\n\\ln \\mid Q \\mid &= -\\frac{7t}{150} + C \\\\\n\\mid Q \\mid &= e^{-\\frac{7t}{150} + C} \\\\\nQ(t) &= Ae^{-7t/150}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 18$, and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(0) &= 18 \\\\\nAe^{-7 \\cdot 0/150} &= 18 \\\\\nA &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 18e^{-7t/150}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(25) = 18e^{-175/150} \\approx 5.61\\,\\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186114968 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nNote that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $300\\,\\text