nside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129762 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128041 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186129765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375148 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129765 1071 "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 4y$, shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)xdV$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186129765 1071 "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129765 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc029278b96456462e746450c8feeb9c9ec6169a9b36f56915758ffcc1f8dd70.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128047 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186129766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375154 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129766 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 4$ for $x \\le 0$.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)(x^{2} + y^{2})dV$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186129766 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $x\\le 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129766 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/de/de9f30a4f54875e05a8cedb6c73af37ad7146c56ab62d0b2b2d3f3fa359160ca.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128527 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186129846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375294 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129846 1074 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=4.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/c5/c55d5f809462b767122ccc4af97c5b4d243730ed24f655bdc18e35ec57e25ce2.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=\\frac{y}{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1.$ So, we have $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1}.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128533 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186129847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375300 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129847 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y = 1 - x^{2}$ and the line $y =-3$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186129847 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the line $y=-3?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186129847 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/72/726ad8599b89029d3dba8e79ca947af99c17bd8712e551e49fc33d3e597a5cb7.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-3\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-y}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373770 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373889 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186133815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373777 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186133936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374192 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186133936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374199 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186133937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374206 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186133937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374213 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186134144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130990 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186134144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374186 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186134144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186134144 1071 "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = ⟨z^{2}, 2y, x^{2} + y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186134144 1071 "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,2y,\\,{x}^{2}+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186134144 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147493 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374937 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147494 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374953 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375617 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374992 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374999 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144378 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144383 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374973 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374978 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374982 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374987 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} + y = xe^{x}$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_{1}(x) = \\sin x$ and $y_{2}(x) = \\cos x$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$u_{1}\\sin x + u_{2}\\cos x$ {{field-1}}\nwhere $u_{1} = u_{1}(x), u_{2} = u_{2}(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n$u_{1}^{′}(\\sin x)^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(\\cos x)^{′}$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186147783 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\cancel{{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\cancel{{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\cancel{{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\cancel{{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144444 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186147790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375013 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147790 1071 "Given that for $s > ω$,\n$L{\\sinh (ωt)} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} - ω^{2}}, L{\\cosh (ωt)} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} - ω^{2}}$,\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2\\cosh (6t) + \\sinh (6t)$ for $s > 6$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186147790 1071 "Given that for $s>\\omega ,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)$ for $s>6.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186147790 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)} & =L{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)} \\\\  & =2L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega , \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-36},\\,s>6 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36},\\,s>6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =2L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-36}+\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-36}+\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s+6}{{s}^{2}-36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186148145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146594 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186148145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375180 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186148145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186148145 1071 "Given that for $s > 0$,\n$L{\\sin ωt} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, L{\\cos ωt} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}$,\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 8\\cos 3t - 5\\sin 3t$ for $s > 0$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186148145 1071 "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ for $s>0.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186148145 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =L{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}+L{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} \\\\  & =8L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}-5L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =8L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}-5L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}-5\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8s}{{s}^{2}+9}-\\frac{15}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8s-15}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151806 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- ![](images/5a/5aa4f3afde005cff577fd75ebfa69e37861edf2628383b1191bf5c07b30fdde6.png)\n\n- ![](images/4c/4cbf587cfd90afaa031f87215c369495a23d50b886cda984b6a9a83a98ba7f06.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151806 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151806 1074 "The sentence\n\n $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number \n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $\\neg P.$ \n\nThe sentence\n\n$\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number \n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $\\neg Q.$\n\nTherefore, the sentence\n\n $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $\\neg P \\lor \\neg Q.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151806 1074 "The sentence\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nThe sentence\n\n> $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sentence\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg P \\lor \\neg Q\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151807 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151807 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151807 1074 "First, we add a column for\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nRemember that the truth values of\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nare opposite those of $Q$.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nThen, we add a column for\n\n$$\nP \\land \\neg Q\n$$\n\nRemember that\n\n$$\nP \\land \\neg Q\n$$\n\nis true if both $P$ and\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nare true.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\land \\neg Q\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for\n\n$$\n\\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\n$$\n\nRemember that the truth values of\n\n$$\n\\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\n$$\n\nare opposite those of\n\n$$\nP \\land \\neg Q\n$$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\land \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg (P \\land \\neg Q) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151807 1074 "First, we add a column for $\\neg Q.$ Remember that the truth values of $\\neg Q$ are opposite those of $Q.$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nThen, we add a column for $P \\land \\neg Q.$ Remember that $P \\land \\neg Q$ is true if both $P$ and $\\neg Q$ are true.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\land \\neg Q\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for $\\neg ( P \\land \\neg Q ).$ Remember that the truth values of $\\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)$ are opposite those of $P \\land \\neg Q.$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\land \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg (P \\land \\neg Q) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151808 1071 "Simplify the statement $\\neg(\\neg C) \\lor \\neg(\\neg(C \\land D))$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151808 1071 "Simplify the statement $\\neg(\\neg C) \\lor \\neg(\\neg(C \\land D)).$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151808 1074 "Two negations in a row cancel each other out. So:\n\n- we can simplify $\\neg(\\neg C)$ to $C$, and\n- we can also simplify $\\neg(\\neg(C \\land D))$ to $C \\land D$.\n\nTherefore, the given statement can be simplified to\n\n$$\nC \\lor (C \\land D)\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151808 1074 "Two negations in a row cancel each other out. So: \n\n- we can simplify $\\neg(\\neg C)$ to $C,$ and\n\n- we can also simplify $\\neg(\\neg(C \\land D))$ to $C \\land D.$\n\nTherefore, the given statement can be simplified to \n\n$$\nC \\lor (C \\land D).\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151809 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n$P: 1$ is **not** smaller than $2$\n\nWhat statement represents $\\neg P?$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151809 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n> $P: 1$ is **not** smaller than $2$\n\nWhich of following represents $\\neg P$?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151809 1074 "The symbol \"\n\n$$\n\\neg\n$$\n\n\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence.\n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n> It is **not** true that $1$ is **not** smaller than $2$.\n\nIn other words, $1$ is smaller than $2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nmeans the following:\n\n> $1$ is smaller than $2$.\n\nNote that $P$ is **false,** while\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nis **true.**" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151809 1074 "The symbol \"$\\neg$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\nIt is **not** true that $1$ is **not** smaller than $2.$\n\nIn other words, $1$ is smaller than $2.$ Therefore, $\\neg P$ means the following:\n\n$1$ is smaller than $2.$\n\nNote that $P$ is **false,** while $\\neg P$ is **true.**" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151823 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad\nB = \\left\\{  2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A = \\{ 2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n\n- $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n\n- $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151823 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n$A = \\{2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\{2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n2. $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n3. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151823 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={2(1),2(2),2(3),…} \\\\  & ={2,4,6,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={{2}^{-n}∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={{2}^{-1},{2}^{-2},{2}^{-3},…} \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is \n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\{ 2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151823 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n$A|= {2n∣n ∈ N}; = {2(1), 2(2), 2(3), …}; = {2, 4, 6, …}$\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n$B|= {2^{-n}∣n ∈ N}; = {2^{-1}, 2^{-2}, 2^{-3}, …}; = {\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{1}{8}, …}$.\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is\n$A \\cap B = \\{2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset$.\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151824 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\}$ and $\\{ v, w \\}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151824 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $\\{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\}$ and $\\{v, w \\}$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151824 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{ v, w \\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{-1,1,\\sqrt{5}}⊔{v,w} & ={-1,1,\\sqrt{5}}\\cup {v,w} \\\\  & ={-1,1,\\sqrt{5},v,w}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151824 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{v, w \\} = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}}⊔{v, w} &= {-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}}∪{v, w} \\\\\n&= {-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}, v, w}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151825 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $A_1 = \\{ 7,8 \\}$ and $A_2 = \\{ 2,4,8 \\}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151825 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $A_1 = \\{7,8 \\}$ and $A_2 = \\{2,4,8 \\}$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186151825 1074 "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{ 7,8 \\} \\cap \\{ 2,4,8 \\} = \\{ 8 \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}={(7,1),(8,1)} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}={(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & ={7,8}⊔{2,4,8} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & ={(7,1),(8,1)}\\cup {(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)} \\\\  & ={(7,1),(8,1),(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186151825 1074 "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{7,8 \\} \\cap \\{2,4,8 \\} = \\{8 \\} \\neq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}A_{1} & → & A_{1}^{∗} = {(7, 1), (8, 1)} \\\\ A_{2} & → & A_{2}^{∗} = {(2, 2), (4, 2), (8, 2)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1}⊔A_{2} &= {7, 8}⊔{2, 4, 8} \\\\\n&= A_{1}^{∗}∪A_{2}^{∗} \\\\\n&= {(7, 1), (8, 1)}∪{(2, 2), (4, 2), (8, 2)} \\\\\n&= {(7, 1), (8, 1), (2, 2), (4, 2), (8, 2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186152015 1071 "If $\\overline{X} = \\{ a, c, d\\}$ and $\\overline{Y} = \\{ a, e, i, o, u \\},$ then what is $\\overline{ X \\cup Y }?$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186152015 1071 "If $\\overline{X} = \\{a, c, d\\}$ and $\\overline{Y} = \\{a, e, i, o, u \\}$, then what is $\\overline{X \\cup Y}$?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186152015 1074 "De Morgan's law for unions states that\n\n$$\n\\overline{X \\cup Y} \\equiv \\overline{X} \\cap \\overline{Y}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X\\cup Y} & =\\overset{―}{X}\\cap \\overset{―}{Y} \\\\  & ={a,c,d}\\cap {a,e,i,o,u} \\\\  & ={a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186152015 1074 "De Morgan's law for unions states that\n$\\overline{X \\cup Y} \\equiv \\overline{X} \\cap \\overline{Y}$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX∪Y^(―) &= X^(―)∩Y^(―) \\\\\n&= {a, c, d}∩{a, e, i, o, u} \\\\\n&= {a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186152016 1071 "If $\\overline{P} = \\{ 2, 4, 6 \\}$ and $\\overline{Q} = \\{ 1, 2, 4 \\},$ then what is $\\overline{ P \\cap Q }?