5585 true] [17592186157621 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186157621 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n$x = u^{2} + v^{2}, y =-v$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_{2}$ under the action of $T$?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186157621 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,-v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,-v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}+1,\\,-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}+1,\\,y=-v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+1,\\,y=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=(-y)^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=x-1\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}+{0}^{2},-0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}+{1}^{2},-1)=(2,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$\n\n![](images/ad/ade48b3f14ed83460a5d818f744103edd75f8394a3f3396a0ff30364bda36c9b.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186157686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154573 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186157686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374551 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186157686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186157686 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(v+1),\\,y=4u(v-u).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186157686 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n$x = u(v + 1), y = 4u(v - u)$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_{3}$ under the action of $T$?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186157686 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u(v+1),\\,4u(v-u)) \\\\  & =(u(1+1),\\,4u(1-u)) \\\\  & =(2u,\\,4u(1-u)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=4u(1-u),\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=4t(1-t),\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny=2x(1-\\frac{x}{2})\\,⇒\\,y=-(x-1)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich is a downward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to ((1)(1+1),\\,4(1)(1-1))=(2,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to ((0)(1+1),\\,4(0)(1-0))=(0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,0)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/97/970003245107516d9be7b4729d6c18abf01d1f48c82211402f06d13cec4e3a08.png)" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186166890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374226 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186166890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374233 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186166890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374240 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186166891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374247 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186166966 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375037 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186166966 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375040 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186166966 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375061 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375043 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186167142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163900 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186167142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374220 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186167142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186167142 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = 2(x^{2} + y^{2})$ on the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186167142 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ on the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186167142 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=9\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\cdot 81\\pi =9.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186175936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375438 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188000 1071 "Since the year $2000$, a herd of antelopes multiplies at a rate proportional to their population. Also, the number of antelopes in the herd decreases by $20$ antelopes per year due to predators. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the population of antelopes at time $t$, where $t$ is the time (in years) since the year $2000$?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186188000 1071 "Since the year $2000$, a herd of antelopes multiplies at a rate proportional to their population. Also, the number of antelopes in the herd decreases by $20$ antelopes per year due to predators. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the population of antelopes at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time (in years) since the year $2000?$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188000 1074 "The rate of change in the number of antelopes per year increases in proportion to $P$ and decreases by $20$. The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t}=kP - 20\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186188000 1074 "The rate of change in the number of antelopes per year increases in proportion to $P$ and decreases by $20.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t}=kP - 20,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188001 1071 "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate inversely proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t)$, in this process." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186188001 1071 "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate inversely proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188001 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ *increases* at a rate *inversely proportional to its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{k}{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{r} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{(\\frac{k}{r})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi {r}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{4\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{K}{r^3}.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188001 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ *increases* at a rate *inversely proportional to its radius.* Therefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{k}{r}$,\nwhere $k > 0$.\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{4}{3}πr^{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 4πr^{2}\\frac{dr}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{r} \\\\\n4πr^{2}\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{r} \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= ((\\frac{k}{r}))/(4πr^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{4πr^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{4\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{K}{r^3}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186188002 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative $y'(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-1,2)$ equals $2$. What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186188002 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative $y'(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-1,2)$ equals $2.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188002 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** $y'(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance from $P$ to the origin.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $x^2+y^2.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional to* the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\ny'(x) = k\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\ny'(x) = \\dfrac{k}{x^2+y^2}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that $y'(x) = 2$ when at the point $(-1, 2).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\ny'(x)= \\dfrac{10}{x^2+y^2}.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188002 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** $y'(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance from $P$ to the origin.***\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $x^2+y^2$.\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional to* the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have\n$y'(x) = k\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}$\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\ny'(x) = \\dfrac{k}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that $y'(x) = 2$ when at the point $(-1, 2)$. We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= (k)/((-1)^{2} + 2^{2}) \\\\\n2 &= \\frac{k}{5} \\\\\nk &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\ny'(x)= \\dfrac{10}{x^2+y^2}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186188209 1071 "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t)$, in this process." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186188209 1071 "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188209 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kr\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi r^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-kr \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-kr \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{2\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = -K.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188209 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to its *radius.* In other words,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kr$\nwhere $k > 0$.\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by\n$A=\\pi r^2$.\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(πr^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= 2πr\\frac{dr}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= -kr \\\\\n2πr\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -kr \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -\\frac{kr}{2πr} \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -\\frac{k}{2π}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{2\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = -K\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186188263 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373826 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188263 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373833 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188263 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373840 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188263 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373847 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188263 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373854 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373721 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375088 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375095 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373714 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373805 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373812 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373819 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375411 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373798 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373861 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373868 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373875 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186188298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373882 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186198021 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{y-x}{x+y} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y$,\nwhere $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4 \\right\\}$." 13194139533451 17592186045585 false] [17592186198021 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{y-x}{x+y} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4 \\right\\}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186198021 1074 "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n![](images/03/039187200f0587407c67e8663de037df0a9e71155e91c4ea497e9d10d66d9119.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n![](images/e7/e7adae8ec845db2e091e9813a338b05895ebca800371df4841d327739863b863.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186198021 1074 "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D$,\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 1 \\le v \\le 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{y - x}{x + y}dxdy &= ∬_(Δ)\\frac{u}{v}\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) & dudv \\\\ = ∬_(Δ)\\frac{u}{v} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix}du