139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373685 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373685 1084 #uuid "f0275cf0-0010-5392-bf0f-cca0e7e11e9c" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373687 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373686 1069 "q-116763" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373686 1071 "![](images/a3/a33571e46d18121fa46fce9cb0acdf9eb4c2a217aeaa19b36b9502bdc66772a6.png)\n\nThe edges of a cube decrease at a rate of $4\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the volume decreasing when the length of each edge is $26\\text{ cm}?$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373686 1072 #uuid "c9b2c993-0665-51c8-8d5d-30fce0e91f5e" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373686 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=26\\text{ cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=26$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3(26)^{2}\\cdot (-4)\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\,112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $8\\,112\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=26\\,\\text{cm}.$" 1319413953345