138" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373861 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-3,4)$ equals $15.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373861 1072 #uuid "6b00489a-807a-5221-8dab-d57f2fcb225b" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373861 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=15$ when at the point $(-3,4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =k\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\ 15 & =k\\cdot 5 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373863 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373864 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373865 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373866 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373867 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373862 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186373862 1077 "