}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of solution in the tank remains $260\\,\\text{L}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(13\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(13\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{20} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{20})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{20}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/20+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/20},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for