′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of solution in the tank remains $375\\,\\text{L}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{375}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{375}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{375}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{375}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{25} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{25})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{25}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/25+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/25},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25} & =9 \\\\ A & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=9{e}^{-t/25}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374830 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374831 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374830 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374830 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374830 1079 #edb/ref 17592186374831 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374830 1080 #uuid "28133954-7d6d-50c3-9942-b8e9c9a1182b" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374831 1081 "9{e}^{-t/25}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374831 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374831 1084 #uuid "7bf9c62e-c4f9-5e3c-8ed7-61d9f3e8d86a" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374833 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374832 1069 "q-336632" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374832 1071 "A tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $