) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine.\n\n- Brine enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $15t\\,\\text{gal}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{gal}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-20t=(600-5t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{600-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{120-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{4Q}{120-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{120-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 175921860455