,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants exit the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminants leave the basin, we first determine the volume of wastewater in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $480\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater; water enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of wastewater in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n480+24t-18t=(480+6t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminants in the basin, the *concentration* of the wastewater at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminants* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{3}{80+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{3}{80+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{80+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{80+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(80+t)^{3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $12\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminants. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=12,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(80+0)^{3}} & =12 \\\\ A & =12\\cdot {80}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12{(\\frac{80}{80+t})}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminants in the basin $10$ minutes after the clean water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(12)=12{(\\frac{80}{90})}^{3}≈8.43\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374848 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374849 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374850 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374851 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374852 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1077 "selection" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1079 #edb/ref 17592186374848 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374847 1080 #uuid "cf9b1d72-7dcf-5756-8025-f1f5167127d2" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374848 1081 "8.43\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374848 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374848 1084 #uuid "22446d9b-f3b3-51d7-8eab-f813f126f966" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374849 1081 "7.54\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374849 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374849 1084 #uuid "fc791120-4af1-5df5-8a8b-8cf249933b52" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374850 1081 "8.22\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374850 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374850 1084 #uuid "2a979bc1-8055-5284-be10-31b92bc2fb71" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374851 1081 "8.08\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374851 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374851 1084 #uuid "b2ab7b77-90c7-560a-a520-39ae98136e1f" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374852 1081 "8.70\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374852 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374852 1084 #uuid "5fb6f7cb-44c0-5462-90c0-5e816014480d" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374854 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374853 1069 "q-336726" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374853 1071 "A $240\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity tank initially holds $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. Fresh water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine flows out at a rate of $2\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374853 1072 #uuid "dcfe7bdc-5ce2-5316-9de6-7dc380d1901a" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374853 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (3\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; fresh water enters at $3\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed brine leaves at $2\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=150+3t-2t=(150+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{150+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $2\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(2\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{150+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(2\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{150+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{150+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{150+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{150+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{150+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{150+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{150+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|150+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(150+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(150+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(150+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=32,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(150+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ A & =32(150)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{32(150)^{2}}{(150+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{150}{150+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=240$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 150+{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =90\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(90) & =32{(\\frac{150}{150+90})}^{2} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{150}{240})}^{2} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{2} \\\\  & =12.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374855 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374856 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374857 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374858 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374859 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1077 "selection" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1079 #edb/ref 17592186374858 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374854 1080 #uuid "94ee6f2e-5f4c-5a4b-80d2-dd6fa77ca97a" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374855 1081 "32\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374855 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374855 1084 #uuid "330391ea-80fb-555c-8100-9cf56bac72be" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374856 1081 "8\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374856 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374856 1084 #uuid "5345a1b0-bd58-534b-b7e1-91989f0e42ae" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374857 1081 "15\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374857 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374857 1084 #uuid "ec22cd8b-9dd7-517c-8622-92daa295ef50" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374858 1081 "12.5\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374858 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374858 1084 #uuid "b3caacbd-86cd-5fc7-86f1-4bebadf1735e" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374859 1081 "16.5\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374859 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374859 1084 #uuid "47645fa8-cc54-5919-9b81-fbaad91413e8" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374861 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374860 1069 "q-336727" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374860 1071 "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity reactor initially holds $7\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of a chemical solution containing $98\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. Pure solvent begins to flow into the reactor at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed solution flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374860 1072 #uuid "45ce4d33-a7f8-5fd0-bbf2-c53f0966bb82" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374860 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $7\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,500+90t-60t=30(250+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{250+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{250+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{250+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{250+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{250+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{250+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|250+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(250+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(250+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(250+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $98\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=98,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =98 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(250+0)^{2}} & =98 \\\\ A & =98(250)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{98(250)^{2}}{(250+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{250}{250+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(250+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 250+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =98{(\\frac{250}{250+100})}^{2} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{250}{350})}^{2} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =50\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374862 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374863 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374864 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374865 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374866 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1077 "selection" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1079 #edb/ref 17592186374863 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374861 1080 #uuid "86cb9083-a221-51ed-ad2c-ae6d13e72d9a" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374862 1081 "48\\,\\text{g}" 13194139533451 175921860