ue] [17592186374888 1072 #uuid "8ee2050b-6343-5391-af1d-62fa3677d5ac" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374888 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=3C{x}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(3C{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =6Cx \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3Cx}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=3C{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{3(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}})x}{y}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: For the original family, since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ whenever $x\\ne 0,$ hence, the slopes are well-defined. Otherwise, when $x=0,$ the curve forces $y=0,$ and the tangent slope is not defined." 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374890 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374891 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374892 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374893 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374894 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374889 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533451 17592186045585 