amily of curves given by ${y}^{2}=Cx.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(Cx) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =C \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{C}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=Cx\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})}{2y}=\\frac{y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{2x})}=-\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-2x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-2x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-2\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-{x}^{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+2{x}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=Cx,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/039a42b728cad07b3e8f5dfd2f1607b36819e586f40e54e06d942679bb4ededa.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family (except possibly at $x=0$), so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined away from $y=0.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374931 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374932 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374931 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374933 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186374931 1075 #edb/ref 17592186