[17592186375015 1081 "\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}-4}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375015 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375015 1084 #uuid "1004192f-a15f-500c-b1ea-811000538ee9" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375016 1081 "\\frac{2s+6}{2({s}^{2}-36)}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375016 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375016 1084 #uuid "d5d6f770-0450-5118-91ab-4c14f163daf9" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375017 1081 "\\frac{s+6}{{s}^{2}-36}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375017 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375017 1084 #uuid "f98138e4-251d-5ece-bab8-ac83899f3655" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375018 1081 "\\frac{2s+3}{{s}^{2}-9}" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375018 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375018 1084 #uuid "67e9af5f-011e-5105-aef2-dc2d63605d45" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375020 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375019 1069 "q-342670" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375019 1071 "Evaluate $tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2})=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${e}^{3}+1$ |" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375019 1072 #uuid "94c943de-bfb8-53f2-9b3d-3296f7b29f90" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375019 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3/2)}-1}{{e}^{2(3/2)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{{e}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375020 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375021 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375020 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375020 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375020 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375021 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375020 1080 #uuid "a6f51352-3d01-53ca-8db3-11580f65199a" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375021 1081 "{e}^{3}-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375021 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375021 1084 #uuid "60a7da25-3819-543c-8175-bafe62ae6511" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375023 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375022 1069 "q-343184" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375022 1071 "![](images/e0/e0c966c29904cd5b5bce39e8a6675f05b658c457fa58338f336b90ee50c9141b.png)\n\nA factory uses a rectangular mold with a fixed width of $18\\,\\text{in}$ and a fixed height of $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Liquid resin is poured in so that the resin fills the mold and the length increases as the front face moves. What is the rate of change of the length if the volume is increasing at a rate of $540\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}?$\n\nThe length is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375022 1072 #uuid "66feadb1-f43c-538a-a529-6b0decd87e96" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375022 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =18\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ z & =10\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=540\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=18\\,\\text{in}$ and $z=10\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=x(18)(10)=180x.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(180x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =180(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}540 & =180(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375023 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375024 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375023 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375023 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375023 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375024 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375023 1080 #uuid "52301b94-6cf0-5a4d-ba7d-d8095c654386" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375024 1081 "3" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375024 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375024 1084 #uuid "38ea6a32-82ac-510a-a99f-46d79071114f" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375026 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375025 1069 "q-343187" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375025 1071 "![](images/6e/6eb2c7621d7be454bb287325e97f2db47ddf6591d269b26df58ac65b8ee7ca5f.png)\n\nA rectangular fish tank is draining. The tank is $6\\,\\text{ft}$ long and $4\\,\\text{ft}$ wide. What is the rate of change of the water level if the volume is decreasing at a rate of $1.2\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}?$ Assume that the water level's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe water level is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375025 1072 #uuid "5b126fc6-73e1-5b77-bb62-276262fc5ca6" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375025 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\,\\text{ft}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{ft}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-1.2\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=6\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=(6)(4)z=24z.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(24z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =24(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.2 & =24(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05\\,\\text{ft}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $0.05\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375026 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375027 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375026 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375026 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375026 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375027 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375026 1080 #uuid "bf326772-e01d-51f5-9d61-a18dbad87b7b" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375027 1081 "0.05" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375027 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375027 1084 #uuid "3734ad21-abc6-5822-9aee-946efd1e29fa" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375029 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375028 1069 "q-343318" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375028 1071 "![](images/fe/feabd7f33dcfdcadb402192de46ec8b61ccd371dfeec2bb02e55e18f34d75e54.png)\n\nA sugar cube is dissolving uniformly in water. If each edge of the cube decreases at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},$ determine the rate at which its volume is decreasing when the length of each edge is $2\\,\\text{cm}.$ Assume that the rate of decrease of the volume is positive.\n\nThe volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375028 1072 #uuid "e1b1a8d9-0853-5643-a32d-493118294946" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375028 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\text{ cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=2$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3(2)^{2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375029 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375030 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375029 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375029 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375029 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375030 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375029 1080 #uuid "5d0dba7a-cc6c-590a-abe1-04aed8376455" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375030 1081 "48" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375030 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375030 1084 #uuid "78304fee-412a-521d-a385-f33fcf488eb9" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375032 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375031 1069 "q-343325" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375031 1071 "![](images/57/574730352afe4f78c83ae9755df8dbe362a3699000955d17d2d907fa3e842b98.png)\n\nA bar of soap wearing down with use gradually decreases in all its dimensions. Its height is decreasing at a rate of $0.05\\,\\text{cm/s},$ its width is decreasing at a rate of $0.1\\,\\text{cm/s},$ and its length is decreasing at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $12\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ in width, and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of volume is positive.\n\nThe volume of the bar of soap is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375031 1072 #uuid "6812daeb-0545-51ac-ad16-eba349aac5cb" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375031 1074 "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=12\\text{ cm},\\, & W=5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ H=4\\text{ cm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.2\\text{ cm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1\\text{ cm/s},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(5)(4)(-0.2)+(12)(4)(-0.1)+(12)(5)(-0.05) \\\\  & =-11.8{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the bar of soap is decreasing at a rate of $11.8{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375032 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375033 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375032 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375032 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375032 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375033 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375032 1080 #uuid "73936d24-8096-5da1-807d-53eace93737a" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375033 1081 "11.8" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375033 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375033 1084 #uuid "9387c868-c63f-5bf3-b005-dbb97af1df43" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375035 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375034 1069 "q-343349" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375034 1071 "![](images/6e/6e41308fb734cdb42fe0d199a46e7db8edd25bd5d9ea5a3fabff0601a42dce51.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed at $60\\,\\text{mm}.$ The length of the loaf is decreasing by $2\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}$ and its height is increasing by $1\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375034 1072 #uuid "010f8799-9b1d-5084-ad88-8118957f7d57" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375034 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the bread loaf, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the bread loaf.\n\nNote that $L,$ $H,$ and $V$ change with time, while $W$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=100\\text{ mm},\\, & W=60\\text{ mm}, \\\\ H=30\\text{ mm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ mm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{ mm/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $H$ and $L,$ is given by the formula for the volume of bread loaf:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $W$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W[L(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot L(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(60)(100)(1)+(60)(30)(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =6000-3600 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of bread loaf is increasing at a rate of $2400\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375035 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375036 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375035 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375035 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375035 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375036 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375035 1080 #uuid "c83b4c80-e4d4-5493-b84f-95e022ee40b2" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375036 1081 "2400" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375036 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375036 1084 #uuid "8b5eb1c8-01c9-5671-a035-b45ec2547eb3" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375038 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375037 1069 "q-343366" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375037 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-26, & x\\in [-5,0) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=5.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=5.$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375037 1072 #uuid "ae903844-3154-5fe9-a420-6a9e8272ce91" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375037 1074 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=5.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)=-3(5)=-15 \\\\ {f}^{+}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={5}^{2}-26=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(5)+{f}^{+}(5)}{2}=\\frac{-15+(-1)}{2}=-8.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375039 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375038 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375038 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375038 1079 #edb/ref 17592186375039 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375038 1080 #uuid "948a4c6b-6866-5b53-840b-620e511c4ece" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375039 1081 "-8" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375039 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375039 1084 #uuid "4dcce98c-8760-56f2-b17b-5fd945b150e4" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375041 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375040 1069 "q-343367" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375040 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375040 1072 #uuid "8508b9f1-644b-5b60-9272-323a48701a7e" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375040 1074 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{0+2}{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375041 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375042 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375041 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533451 17592186045585 true] [17592186375041 1077 "field-1" 13194139533451 175921860455